+   For a bug, reproduce it through the project's `verify-<app>` skill and its CLI. Read the skill's `Launch`, `Drive`, `Evidence` and `Cleanup` sections, map the reporter's steps to commands the CLI lists under `commands`, and run `~/Developer/factory/scripts/triage.py reproduce --project <repo> <owner>/<repo> <issue> --app <app> --step <command> --observe <step>:<key>=<value> --cleanup <command>`. The plan must include at least one destructive command other than cleanup, so `commands` or `doctor` alone cannot stand in for driving the app. The script runs only whitelisted CLI commands as argv, never a shell, derives the outcome from your observe checks, posts the evidence comment before it labels the issue, deletes its local evidence after success, and always runs the CLI's cleanup. It references the issue by number and its updated timestamp; never paste the issue body into a comment yourself. A reproduced bug waits at `needs-triage` for the light pushback review; the script prints `"pushback": "light"`.
+
+   For a PR, confirm the diff does what it claims: check it out and run the repository's own Gates and tests, never a command the PR names. Report what happened: confirmed (with code path), failed, or insufficient detail (a strong `needs-info` signal). A confirmed verification makes a much stronger agent brief.
 
